(() => { "use strict"; const panels = {"FV-01": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-01\"><summary>FV-01 · Confirm the effect before retrying</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> The assistant has stopped talking, but the payment might still be unfinished.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-01-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-01-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A claims agent receives stop_reason=tool_use with two read-only calls, IDs u1 and u2. Both calls finish. What should its Messages orchestration loop do next?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> End this model loop and check operation status through the application recovery path before retrying the write.</li><li><b>B.</b> Repeat the most recent write with a new operation ID.</li><li><b>C.</b> Treat any assistant text as proof of completion.</li><li><b>D.</b> Return each result with its matching tool_use_id and send the next model request.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> The payment receipt, not the assistant’s final message, tells you whether money moved.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A support agent has one refund tool call pending. Return tool_result with its matching tool_use_id and continue the conversation; keep the business transaction status separately.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A network timeout leaves operation r-17 unknown. Query r-17 status or reconcile by idempotency key before sending another refund.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>stop_reason=tool_use; pending_id=t17; operation_id=r17; business_status=unknown</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: end_turn ends the model turn, while an unknown business effect still needs reconciliation. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A new operation ID can create a duplicate effect.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Text is not a reliable stop_reason or a transaction receipt.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: The protocol requires results for the requested calls before the next model iteration.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A blind refund retry can duplicate a committed transaction. Model termination is not proof of settlement.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.1.html#learn-fv-01\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-01-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-01-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A settlement agent receives end_turn after a write returned status=unknown. There are no pending tool calls, but business completion has not been established. What should the application do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Treat any assistant text as proof of completion.</li><li><b>B.</b> Return each result with its matching tool_use_id and send the next model request.</li><li><b>C.</b> End this model loop and check operation status through the application recovery path before retrying the write.</li><li><b>D.</b> Repeat the most recent write with a new operation ID.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>end_turn ends the model turn, while an unknown business effect still needs reconciliation.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Text is not a reliable stop_reason or a transaction receipt.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: The protocol requires results for the requested calls before the next model iteration. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: end_turn ends the model turn, while an unknown business effect still needs reconciliation.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: A new operation ID can create a duplicate effect.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.1&amp;variant=FV-01\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.1.html#learn-fv-01\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-02": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-02\"><summary>FV-02 · Cover dimensions before synthesizing</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A comparison looks finished even though one required topic has no evidence.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-02-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-02-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A law firm requests a vendor comparison covering retention, jurisdiction and operational recovery. Its draft decomposition investigates only three vendors’ prices. What is the best correction?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Always invoke every available specialist regardless of the request.</li><li><b>B.</b> Let each specialist independently publish its own final answer.</li><li><b>C.</b> Use the bounded single-agent lookup and return the supported answer.</li><li><b>D.</b> Have a coordinator allocate the missing comparison dimensions, collect attributed findings and check coverage.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Keep a checklist of the requested topics. Ask for the missing evidence before calling the comparison complete.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A legal team compares three vendors on evidence retention, integration cost and deployment permissions. Assign dimensions, require source-tagged findings and let the coordinator integrate the comparison.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Cost returns, but retention is missing. Re-delegate retention with an explicit gap; do not publish a complete comparison.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>coverage={retention:pending,cost:complete,permissions:complete}; owner=coordinator</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Invoking a fixed roster does not establish coverage and adds unnecessary work.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: The user needs a coherent synthesis with one owner for conflicts and omissions.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: The bounded question is already supported without specialist coordination overhead. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Dimension-based coverage repairs the omitted requirements.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Assigning one vendor to each specialist can hide inconsistent criteria unless the return contracts enforce the same dimensions.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.2.html#learn-fv-02\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-02-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-02-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A lawyer asks for the current retention period of one named policy. One read-only lookup returns the authoritative clause and date. What is the best orchestration choice?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Have a coordinator allocate the missing comparison dimensions, collect attributed findings and check coverage.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use the bounded single-agent lookup and return the supported answer.</li><li><b>C.</b> Always invoke every available specialist regardless of the request.</li><li><b>D.</b> Let each specialist independently publish its own final answer.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>The bounded question is already supported without specialist coordination overhead.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Dimension-based coverage repairs the omitted requirements. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: The bounded question is already supported without specialist coordination overhead.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Invoking a fixed roster does not establish coverage and adds unnecessary work.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: The user needs a coherent synthesis with one owner for conflicts and omissions.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.2&amp;variant=FV-02\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.2.html#learn-fv-02\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-03": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-03\"><summary>FV-03 · Send an attributable handoff</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A helper is asked to use information it has never received.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-03-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-03-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A synthesis specialist is isolated from its parent. Earlier agents found campaign claims and conflicting source dates. Which handoff should the coordinator provide?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Supply the question, scoped findings, source locators, dates and expected output contract explicitly.</li><li><b>B.</b> Tell the specialist to use everything the parent already knows.</li><li><b>C.</b> Send only source titles without findings or locators.</li><li><b>D.</b> Dispatch both independent investigations together with bounded scopes and quality criteria.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Send the actual question, allowed evidence and expected return fields. A helper cannot read another conversation just because you named it.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A marketing specialist checks audience evidence. Send the precise question, permitted data, exclusions, cited upstream findings and output contract, not a reference to another unseen chat.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The specialist claims it used a result never included in its brief. Reject that claim and resend the bounded evidence packet.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>brief={question:segment_fit,scope:consented_records,evidence:[source:CRM42],return:[finding,source,limits]}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Explicit context and attribution enable an isolated specialist to reason from prior evidence.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Isolated specialist context does not automatically include parent conversation history.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Titles alone cannot preserve the findings or let the specialist verify conflicting claims.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: Independent investigations can run in parallel without inventing a dependency. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Shared task naming is not shared conversation context; a bare “use the earlier findings” instruction is insufficient.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.3.html#learn-fv-03\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-03-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-03-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>Two independent channel audits must finish before synthesis; neither needs the other’s output. What should the coordinator do at dispatch?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Supply the question, scoped findings, source locators, dates and expected output contract explicitly.</li><li><b>B.</b> Dispatch both independent investigations together with bounded scopes and quality criteria.</li><li><b>C.</b> Tell the specialist to use everything the parent already knows.</li><li><b>D.</b> Send only source titles without findings or locators.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Independent investigations can run in parallel without inventing a dependency.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Explicit context and attribution enable an isolated specialist to reason from prior evidence. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Independent investigations can run in parallel without inventing a dependency.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Isolated specialist context does not automatically include parent conversation history.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Titles alone cannot preserve the findings or let the specialist verify conflicting claims.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.3&amp;variant=FV-03\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.3.html#learn-fv-03\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-04": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-04\"><summary>FV-04 · Bind approval to this action</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Someone approved one payment; the account or amount then changed.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-04-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-04-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A transfer approval is bound to account A, amount 800 and proposal version 3. The agent changes the destination and produces version 4. What should the execution gate do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Escalate with verified identity, unresolved issue, evidence, proposal version and operation status.</li><li><b>B.</b> Reject execution until the changed proposal receives current scoped authorization.</li><li><b>C.</b> Use the old approval because the customer was verified earlier.</li><li><b>D.</b> Finish the transfer first, then provide a summary to a human.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Approval belongs to the particular action that was checked. The application must check the current action before it runs.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A finance support workflow proposes a 40-unit credit for account A and proposal v2. Trusted verification, scope and current approval are checked by the action handler before the effect.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Verification expires between draft and execute. Reject the protected action and hand off the complete case with blocked status.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>gate={account:A,proposal:v2,amount:40,verified:true,approval:current}; requested={account:B,proposal:v2}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: A complete handoff lets the human continue without guessing current state. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Authorization for a different proposal cannot justify the new effect.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Identity verification is not approval of every later account/action/version.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: An unresolved human request does not authorize an irreversible action.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A prompt reminding Claude to verify cannot enforce the backend boundary. Read-only investigation follows its own access policy.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.4.html#learn-fv-04\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-04-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-04-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A bank customer explicitly requests a human while a transfer dispute is unresolved. The receiving officer cannot read the transcript. What should the agent do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use the old approval because the customer was verified earlier.</li><li><b>B.</b> Escalate with verified identity, unresolved issue, evidence, proposal version and operation status.</li><li><b>C.</b> Finish the transfer first, then provide a summary to a human.</li><li><b>D.</b> Reject execution until the changed proposal receives current scoped authorization.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A complete handoff lets the human continue without guessing current state.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Identity verification is not approval of every later account/action/version.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A complete handoff lets the human continue without guessing current state.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: An unresolved human request does not authorize an irreversible action.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: Authorization for a different proposal cannot justify the new effect. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.4&amp;variant=FV-04\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.4.html#learn-fv-04\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-05": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-05\"><summary>FV-05 · Separate prevention from detection</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A check notices a problem only after the action has happened.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-05-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-05-base-v1</small></p><p><b>Two document tools return ISO dates and epoch timestamps. Raw evidence must remain auditable, and no external write is involved. Which mechanism best normalizes what the model receives?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Check the hold in a pre-action application gate or blocking pre-tool hook before deletion.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use a post-tool validator to claim the deletion was prevented.</li><li><b>C.</b> Rely only on an instruction saying to be careful with legal holds.</li><li><b>D.</b> Normalize returned data in a post-tool transformation while preserving raw values and provenance.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> A check before the action can prevent it. A check afterward can detect it and start recovery, but cannot make the action never have happened.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A fictional service normalizes a successful date result after the tool returns while retaining the original value and parse status.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A post-result check finds a bad write already happened. Record the effect and invoke reviewed compensation or escalation; result replacement does not undo it.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>trace=proposal -&gt; precheck -&gt; execute -&gt; effect -&gt; postcheck; raw_date=03/04; locale=unknown</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: A prevention requirement must be enforced before the side effect. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A post-action check cannot prevent an action that has already succeeded.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Probabilistic prompt compliance cannot enforce the stated mandatory gate.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Post-tool normalization is appropriate for already returned read data.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A post-action hook can change model-visible output where supported, but cannot retroactively block the completed effect.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.5.html#learn-fv-05\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-05-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-05-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A records deletion must never execute when a legal hold is active. The current proposal checks only after the delete tool succeeds. Which design fixes the timing?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Normalize returned data in a post-tool transformation while preserving raw values and provenance.</li><li><b>B.</b> Check the hold in a pre-action application gate or blocking pre-tool hook before deletion.</li><li><b>C.</b> Rely only on an instruction saying to be careful with legal holds.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use a post-tool validator to claim the deletion was prevented.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A prevention requirement must be enforced before the side effect.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Post-tool normalization is appropriate for already returned read data. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A prevention requirement must be enforced before the side effect.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Probabilistic prompt compliance cannot enforce the stated mandatory gate.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: A post-action check cannot prevent an action that has already succeeded.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.5&amp;variant=FV-05\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.5.html#learn-fv-05\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-06": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-06\"><summary>FV-06 · Choose decomposition by uncertainty</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> The task may be a known sequence or an investigation whose next step depends on findings.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-06-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-06-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A campaign compliance review always checks each file against three known criteria and then checks cross-file consistency. Which decomposition fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use one unstructured pass over all files and rely on overall impressions.</li><li><b>B.</b> Start an adaptive investigation that expands tasks as evidence reveals relevant dependencies.</li><li><b>C.</b> Stop after a fixed three steps even if an acceptance criterion remains unresolved.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use predictable per-file review stages followed by a cross-file integration pass.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Use fixed steps when the path is known. Allow bounded exploration when new evidence determines the path; define when to stop.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A government intake follows a known sequence: validate, classify, route. Use an explicit workflow with defined outputs and stop states.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> After ten iterations the agent has activity but no evidence for one requirement. Stop or re-plan using the coverage ledger, not iteration count alone.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>steps=[validate,classify,route]; done={valid:true,route_assigned:true}; missing=[]</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: An undifferentiated pass obscures local findings and cross-file contradictions.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Unknown causes require investigation that responds to discoveries. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: A step count is a guardrail, not evidence that investigation is complete.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Known stages support a fixed review chain plus integration.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Multiple agents do not cure an unclear completion contract. Fixed steps are easier to test when the process is known.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.6.html#learn-fv-06\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-06-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-06-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A campaign launch failure has an unknown cause; each log finding may expose a new dependency. Which decomposition fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use predictable per-file review stages followed by a cross-file integration pass.</li><li><b>B.</b> Stop after a fixed three steps even if an acceptance criterion remains unresolved.</li><li><b>C.</b> Start an adaptive investigation that expands tasks as evidence reveals relevant dependencies.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use one unstructured pass over all files and rely on overall impressions.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Unknown causes require investigation that responds to discoveries.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Known stages support a fixed review chain plus integration. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A step count is a guardrail, not evidence that investigation is complete.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Unknown causes require investigation that responds to discoveries.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: An undifferentiated pass obscures local findings and cross-file contradictions.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.6&amp;variant=FV-06\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.6.html#learn-fv-06\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-07": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-07\"><summary>FV-07 · Resume with fresh permissions</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A paused investigation restarts after files or access changed.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-07-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-07-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A resumed contract session points to an older file revision; the repository revision and user permissions changed overnight. What must happen before relying on its findings?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Fork from the baseline and keep branch conclusions separately attributable.</li><li><b>B.</b> Replace missing source text with the prior summary’s likely wording.</li><li><b>C.</b> Assume resuming makes previous tool results current.</li><li><b>D.</b> Reacquire changed files and permissions, retaining only still-valid evidence.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Remembering the old conversation does not make old facts or permissions current. Check what changed before continuing.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> An interrupted legal repository investigation resumes with source pointers and a checkpoint. Re-read changed files and revalidate permissions before continuing.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A stored path was renamed and prior access revoked. Reacquire current evidence; do not trust an old tool result just because the session resumed.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>checkpoint={commit:abc,paths:[policy.md],permissions_checked:old}; current_commit:def</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Forking supports alternative exploration while retaining the shared starting point. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A summary is not authority to reconstruct missing source text.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: External state can change independently of saved session context.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Resumption preserves conversation state, not the freshness of external evidence.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Resume and fork preserve or branch state; neither renews authorization nor makes stale evidence current.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.7.html#learn-fv-07\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-07-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-07-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>An analyst wants to compare two independent interpretations from the same verified baseline without overwriting the original investigation. Which session approach fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Assume resuming makes previous tool results current.</li><li><b>B.</b> Replace missing source text with the prior summary’s likely wording.</li><li><b>C.</b> Reacquire changed files and permissions, retaining only still-valid evidence.</li><li><b>D.</b> Fork from the baseline and keep branch conclusions separately attributable.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Forking supports alternative exploration while retaining the shared starting point.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: External state can change independently of saved session context.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A summary is not authority to reconstruct missing source text.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Resumption preserves conversation state, not the freshness of external evidence. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Forking supports alternative exploration while retaining the shared starting point.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=1.7&amp;variant=FV-07\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-1.7.html#learn-fv-07\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-08": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-08\"><summary>FV-08 · Make capabilities distinguishable</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Two tools sound alike, and the assistant selects a write when it only needs to read.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-08-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-08-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A payment catalog has tools named payment_action1 and payment_action2; one drafts, one executes. Their descriptions omit side effects. What change best reduces confusion?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Expose unrestricted action strings and trust the model to choose only safe ones.</li><li><b>B.</b> Add a longer general prompt while retaining ambiguous tool contracts.</li><li><b>C.</b> Keep a separate scoped execution capability with an application authorization check.</li><li><b>D.</b> Give draft and execute distinct names, inputs, side-effect descriptions and prerequisite contracts.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Make each tool’s purpose and limits clear. The application must still reject an unsafe action.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A support catalog separates get_balance, draft_credit and execute_credit. Descriptions specify read versus proposal versus mutation, required scope and return status.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Claude picks execute_credit for a lookup. Reject the call at the handler, then repair the catalog rather than merely lengthening the prompt.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>get_balance(account_id)-&gt;amount; draft_credit(account_id,amount)-&gt;proposal; execute_credit(proposal_id,approval)-&gt;receipt</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: An unrestricted universal capability bypasses the required application boundary.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: General instruction cannot repair ambiguous interface semantics reliably.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: A narrow execution boundary can be enforced independently of model selection. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Distinct tool contracts make operational purpose and requirements explicit.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A universal tool can conceal prohibited effects and confuse selection; splitting should follow real capability boundaries, not arbitrary tool-count ceilings.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.1.html#learn-fv-08\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-08-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-08-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A redesign proposes one universal execute tool that accepts arbitrary action strings. The application must enforce separate read and payment capabilities. Which choice best preserves the boundary?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Give draft and execute distinct names, inputs, side-effect descriptions and prerequisite contracts.</li><li><b>B.</b> Keep a separate scoped execution capability with an application authorization check.</li><li><b>C.</b> Expose unrestricted action strings and trust the model to choose only safe ones.</li><li><b>D.</b> Add a longer general prompt while retaining ambiguous tool contracts.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A narrow execution boundary can be enforced independently of model selection.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Distinct tool contracts make operational purpose and requirements explicit. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A narrow execution boundary can be enforced independently of model selection.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: An unrestricted universal capability bypasses the required application boundary.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: General instruction cannot repair ambiguous interface semantics reliably.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=2.1&amp;variant=FV-08\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.1.html#learn-fv-08\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-09": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-09\"><summary>FV-09 · Route the actual error category</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A tool fails, but the next move depends on the kind of failure.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-09-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-09-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A CRM campaign lookup returns success=true and records=[]. What is the appropriate recovery?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Convert every failure or empty result into a fabricated placeholder record.</li><li><b>B.</b> Treat this as an empty successful lookup and clarify or widen the query if the task requires records.</li><li><b>C.</b> Look up the existing operation by its stable key and reconcile status before deciding whether to retry.</li><li><b>D.</b> Repeat the identical request indefinitely without examining the outcome type.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> A temporary service failure, a bad request and an uncertain completed write need different recovery. Find out what happened before repeating an effect.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> An MCP lookup succeeds with records=[]; report no matching record and clarify filters rather than retrying a supposed outage.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A write times out after submission. Query operation status before retrying; a transient category alone does not establish safe re-execution.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>application_result={status:success,records:[]}; failure={category:permission,retryable:false}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Invented records hide missing data rather than recover it.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: An empty result is not evidence of transport failure.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: A potentially committed write needs status reconciliation to avoid duplicates. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Recovery must depend on error class, retryability and side-effect uncertainty.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> MCP protocol errors and the application payload are different layers; error strings alone do not define a safe recovery policy.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.2.html#learn-fv-09\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-09-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-09-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A campaign creation request times out after transmission; it may have committed and accepts an idempotency key. What should happen before creating another campaign?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Treat this as an empty successful lookup and clarify or widen the query if the task requires records.</li><li><b>B.</b> Convert every failure or empty result into a fabricated placeholder record.</li><li><b>C.</b> Repeat the identical request indefinitely without examining the outcome type.</li><li><b>D.</b> Look up the existing operation by its stable key and reconcile status before deciding whether to retry.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A potentially committed write needs status reconciliation to avoid duplicates.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: An empty result is not evidence of transport failure. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Invented records hide missing data rather than recover it.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Recovery must depend on error class, retryability and side-effect uncertainty.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: A potentially committed write needs status reconciliation to avoid duplicates.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=2.2&amp;variant=FV-09\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.2.html#learn-fv-09\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-10": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-10\"><summary>FV-10 · Scope tools to the assigned role</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A specialist receives more capabilities than its job needs.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-10-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-10-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A clause research specialist only needs read access to a policy corpus. The coordinator also has a deletion tool. Which tool allocation fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Treat forced tool choice as proof that every resulting action is authorized.</li><li><b>B.</b> Reject the unauthorized operation even if invocation and schema validation succeeded.</li><li><b>C.</b> Give every agent all tools because a coordinator exists.</li><li><b>D.</b> Give the specialist the relevant read tools without the unrelated deletion capability.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Give it the tools needed for that job. Requiring a tool call does not prove the arguments are sensible or the action permitted.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Research specialists get only their relevant read tools. A separate authorized executor handles accepted mutations; the coordinator tracks ownership.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Forced tool choice invokes the named tool with invalid semantics. Authorization and application validation still reject it.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>tools={research:[search,read],executor:[approved_write]}; proposal_owner=coordinator</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: tool_choice controls invocation, not authorization or semantic correctness.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Invocation control and shape validation do not confer business authorization. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Coordination does not enforce least privilege by itself.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Minimum relevant capabilities reduce accidental exposure without assuming multi-agent structure alone is safe.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> tool_choice governs invocation; it does not prove business permission, factual truth or a sound result.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.3.html#learn-fv-10\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-10-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-10-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A schema-constrained tool call returns an account identifier that is outside the requester’s authorization. What should the application do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Give the specialist the relevant read tools without the unrelated deletion capability.</li><li><b>B.</b> Reject the unauthorized operation even if invocation and schema validation succeeded.</li><li><b>C.</b> Treat forced tool choice as proof that every resulting action is authorized.</li><li><b>D.</b> Give every agent all tools because a coordinator exists.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Invocation control and shape validation do not confer business authorization.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Minimum relevant capabilities reduce accidental exposure without assuming multi-agent structure alone is safe. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Invocation control and shape validation do not confer business authorization.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: tool_choice controls invocation, not authorization or semantic correctness.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Coordination does not enforce least privilege by itself.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=2.3&amp;variant=FV-10\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.3.html#learn-fv-10\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-11": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-11\"><summary>FV-11 · Separate connection from inclusion</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> An external service connects, but the assistant still does not have the relevant document.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-11-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-11-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A team needs a reviewed MCP server configuration shared with its repository, but each developer has a separate credential. What should be committed?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Assume all server resources are already available to the model once connection succeeds.</li><li><b>B.</b> Commit a developer token so every clone works immediately.</li><li><b>C.</b> Verify host resource discovery, permissions and explicit inclusion rather than assume connection supplied content.</li><li><b>D.</b> Commit safe project-scoped configuration with credentials supplied externally through the supported mechanism.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Connecting to a service and putting a permitted document into the assistant’s working information are separate steps.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A shared marketing repository uses safe project MCP configuration with secrets provided outside committed files. Inspect the actual host’s tool/resource discovery behavior.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The server connects but the expected resource is not in model context. Check listing, host support and explicit inclusion; do not claim automatic availability.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>project_config={server:crm,secret:environment}; trace=[connected,listed,included]</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Resource advertisement is not automatic context inclusion in every host.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Repository distribution would expose the personal credential.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Server availability does not establish host inclusion or model-visible evidence. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Configuration sharing and secret handling are separate responsibilities.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Connectivity is not proof that every resource was discovered or supplied. Resource behavior depends on host support and invocation.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.4.html#learn-fv-11\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-11-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-11-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A connected MCP server advertises a resource. The host has not discovered or included that resource in the agent’s context. What should the architect verify?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Commit a developer token so every clone works immediately.</li><li><b>B.</b> Commit safe project-scoped configuration with credentials supplied externally through the supported mechanism.</li><li><b>C.</b> Verify host resource discovery, permissions and explicit inclusion rather than assume connection supplied content.</li><li><b>D.</b> Assume all server resources are already available to the model once connection succeeds.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Server availability does not establish host inclusion or model-visible evidence.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Repository distribution would expose the personal credential.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Configuration sharing and secret handling are separate responsibilities. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Server availability does not establish host inclusion or model-visible evidence.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Resource advertisement is not automatic context inclusion in every host.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=2.4&amp;variant=FV-11\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.4.html#learn-fv-11\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-12": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-12\"><summary>FV-12 · Search before making a scoped edit</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A broad code replacement touches unrelated behavior.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-12-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-12-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A campaign repository has hundreds of templates. You must find the code emitting an incorrect unsubscribe URL and make a small repair. Which sequence fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Edit a guessed filename without reading its current content.</li><li><b>B.</b> Find relevant paths and text, read the matching code, then edit the smallest supported region and inspect its diff.</li><li><b>C.</b> Write the new artifact at the agreed path and validate its schema.</li><li><b>D.</b> Rewrite every template before locating the failing URL logic.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Find and read the relevant code first. Make the smallest justified edit, then inspect what changed and test it.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> An unfamiliar education repository needs a one-function fix. Glob relevant paths, Grep the symbol, Read callers and tests, then Edit the precise existing region.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A broad replacement changes unrelated handlers. Restore the unintended changes and inspect the focused diff and targeted behavior.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>evidence=[paths,symbol_hits,caller_read,test_read]; change={file:handler.py,region:retry_condition}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Guessed edits can corrupt unrelated content and do not establish cause.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Search and read establish evidence for a scoped edit.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Creating a new artifact is different from patching an existing region. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: A broad rewrite increases change surface without locating the defect.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Dumping or rewriting the repository risks losing context and unrelated code. Tool selection follows the task, not a fixed ritual for every edit.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.5.html#learn-fv-12\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-12-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-12-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>The requested artifact is a brand-new standalone CSV with an explicit schema; no existing file needs modification. Which operation fits after inspecting the target location?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Write the new artifact at the agreed path and validate its schema.</li><li><b>B.</b> Rewrite every template before locating the failing URL logic.</li><li><b>C.</b> Find relevant paths and text, read the matching code, then edit the smallest supported region and inspect its diff.</li><li><b>D.</b> Edit a guessed filename without reading its current content.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Creating a new artifact is different from patching an existing region.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Creating a new artifact is different from patching an existing region.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A broad rewrite increases change surface without locating the defect.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Search and read establish evidence for a scoped edit. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Guessed edits can corrupt unrelated content and do not establish cause.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=2.5&amp;variant=FV-12\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-2.5.html#learn-fv-12\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-13": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-13\"><summary>FV-13 · Put instructions at their real scope</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A rule is placed where the intended team or project will not reliably receive it.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-13-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-13-base-v1</small></p><p><b>Repository conventions must apply to all contributors, while one analyst’s preferred response tone is personal. What instruction placement fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Copy the analyst’s entire personal instruction file into every repository.</li><li><b>B.</b> Keep shared conventions in project instructions and personal preferences in the personal scope.</li><li><b>C.</b> Identify the applicable instruction files and resolve the conflict according to authority before editing.</li><li><b>D.</b> Combine contradictory instructions and silently choose whichever is shorter.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Put shared instructions at shared scope and personal preferences at personal scope. Resolve conflicts using the actual runtime’s rules.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A shared repository convention belongs in project instructions; personal preferences stay in personal configuration. Trace which nested instructions apply to the current path.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Two applicable files disagree on test behavior. Surface and resolve the conflict using the runtime’s documented loading/scope rules.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>instructions={shared:CLAUDE.md,package:services/CLAUDE.md,personal:user}; target=services/api.py</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Personal preferences need not become shared project requirements.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Scope should match who owns and needs the instruction.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: An identified instruction conflict needs explicit resolution rather than accidental behavior. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Silent arbitrary selection makes the result unauditable.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> More copies increase drift. Instruction files guide model behavior; they do not replace enforced permission checks.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.1.html#learn-fv-13\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-13-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-13-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A child directory’s instructions conflict with applicable project policy on retaining evidence. The conflict changes the requested output. What should the agent do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Combine contradictory instructions and silently choose whichever is shorter.</li><li><b>B.</b> Keep shared conventions in project instructions and personal preferences in the personal scope.</li><li><b>C.</b> Identify the applicable instruction files and resolve the conflict according to authority before editing.</li><li><b>D.</b> Copy the analyst’s entire personal instruction file into every repository.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>An identified instruction conflict needs explicit resolution rather than accidental behavior.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Silent arbitrary selection makes the result unauditable.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Scope should match who owns and needs the instruction. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: An identified instruction conflict needs explicit resolution rather than accidental behavior.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Personal preferences need not become shared project requirements.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.1&amp;variant=FV-13\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.1.html#learn-fv-13\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-14": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-14\"><summary>FV-14 · Give reusable skills a contract</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A reusable task runs without an essential input.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-14-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-14-base-v1</small></p><p><b>The team repeatedly produces campaign evidence packets from a supplied brief, source list and output directory. What reusable artifact fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Create a scoped command or skill with documented inputs, dependencies, output contract and explicit missing-input behavior.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use the task brief directly without introducing a reusable workflow solely for this one case.</li><li><b>C.</b> Install a global workflow with embedded project-specific credentials.</li><li><b>D.</b> Create a reusable command that silently invents missing source evidence.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> A reusable instruction needs a clear input and result. If the input is missing, stop and say what is needed rather than inventing it.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A repeated reviewed-release brief becomes a scoped skill with required commit, evidence paths and output expectations. Check discovery and allowed dependencies.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The skill is invoked without the reviewed commit. Stop with a missing-input message instead of inventing a build identity.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>skill_input={reviewed_commit,tests,evidence}; output={changes,limits,next_action}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: A repeatable workflow benefits from explicit invocation and failure contracts.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: A one-off complete brief does not automatically justify maintaining a reusable abstraction. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Global scope and embedded secrets are inappropriate for project-specific credentials.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Missing evidence must remain unresolved rather than fabricated.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A reusable skill is not permission to publish. Invocation and downstream authorization remain separate.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.2.html#learn-fv-14\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-14-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-14-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A user gives one short task with complete inputs that is unlikely to recur. What should the agent create?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Create a reusable command that silently invents missing source evidence.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use the task brief directly without introducing a reusable workflow solely for this one case.</li><li><b>C.</b> Create a scoped command or skill with documented inputs, dependencies, output contract and explicit missing-input behavior.</li><li><b>D.</b> Install a global workflow with embedded project-specific credentials.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A one-off complete brief does not automatically justify maintaining a reusable abstraction.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Missing evidence must remain unresolved rather than fabricated.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A one-off complete brief does not automatically justify maintaining a reusable abstraction.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: A repeatable workflow benefits from explicit invocation and failure contracts. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Global scope and embedded secrets are inappropriate for project-specific credentials.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.2&amp;variant=FV-14\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.2.html#learn-fv-14\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-15": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-15\"><summary>FV-15 · Load rules for the affected paths</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A file moves and the rule intended for it no longer applies.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-15-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-15-base-v1</small></p><p><b>Only files in backend/payments require the payment-specific test convention. What configuration is appropriate?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Assume a rule applies because its filename sounds relevant without checking its path pattern.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use a path-scoped rule whose pattern matches the payment files and verify it is actually loaded for the change.</li><li><b>C.</b> Duplicate payment-only rules into unrelated documentation and UI scopes.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use an applicable broader instruction for the cross-cutting attribution policy.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> A path rule is like a label on a folder: check that the file’s current path still matches, and that unrelated paths do not.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Backend and test conventions are scoped to their respective path patterns. Predict applicable rules for a change touching both areas and verify actual loading.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A test file moved outside the expected glob, so its rule was not loaded. Correct the scope and check a matching and nonmatching path.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>paths=[services/api.py,tests/test_api.py]; rules={backend:services/**,testing:tests/**}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Names alone do not establish rule matching or loading.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Conditional conventions should load for their relevant paths.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Broad duplication increases conflicts without making the payment rule more precise.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: A genuinely cross-cutting requirement needs correspondingly broad applicability. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Duplicating every rule into the main prompt can hide mismatches and flood context; path scope must match actual files.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.3.html#learn-fv-15\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-15-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-15-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>The same repository has a policy requiring evidence attribution in every generated artifact, regardless of path. What rule scope fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Assume a rule applies because its filename sounds relevant without checking its path pattern.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use an applicable broader instruction for the cross-cutting attribution policy.</li><li><b>C.</b> Use a path-scoped rule whose pattern matches the payment files and verify it is actually loaded for the change.</li><li><b>D.</b> Duplicate payment-only rules into unrelated documentation and UI scopes.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A genuinely cross-cutting requirement needs correspondingly broad applicability.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Names alone do not establish rule matching or loading.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A genuinely cross-cutting requirement needs correspondingly broad applicability.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Conditional conventions should load for their relevant paths. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Broad duplication increases conflicts without making the payment rule more precise.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.3&amp;variant=FV-15\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.3.html#learn-fv-15\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-16": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-16\"><summary>FV-16 · Plan when uncertainty crosses boundaries</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A small-looking code change turns out to affect another system.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-16-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-16-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A change spans authentication, ingestion and retention behavior; dependencies and acceptance boundaries are still unclear. Which starting mode fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Start a cross-file rewrite before discovering dependencies.</li><li><b>B.</b> Require an extensive architectural planning phase for every one-character edit.</li><li><b>C.</b> Perform the clear bounded edit directly and verify the rendered label.</li><li><b>D.</b> Inspect dependencies and develop a reviewed plan before making the broad change.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Reconsider the plan when the scope changes. A short request can still require investigation and coordination.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A cross-service authentication change has unclear callers and migration impact. Inspect evidence, produce a plan and review dependencies before editing.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> An apparently small edit reveals a shared API dependency. Pause to revise the plan rather than continuing under the old assumption.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>plan={affected:[client,server,tests],unknown:[migration],acceptance:[old_client_works]}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Premature implementation can violate unknown dependencies and acceptance constraints.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Planning effort should be proportional to uncertainty and impact.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: A small unambiguous low-impact change can be executed and checked directly. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Uncertainty and cross-file impact justify planning and evidence gathering.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Always planning adds overhead to bounded simple work; always executing can miss uncertain dependencies.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.4.html#learn-fv-16\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-16-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-16-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A reviewed typo fix touches one documented label, has an exact replacement and no behavior change. Which starting mode fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Inspect dependencies and develop a reviewed plan before making the broad change.</li><li><b>B.</b> Require an extensive architectural planning phase for every one-character edit.</li><li><b>C.</b> Perform the clear bounded edit directly and verify the rendered label.</li><li><b>D.</b> Start a cross-file rewrite before discovering dependencies.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A small unambiguous low-impact change can be executed and checked directly.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Uncertainty and cross-file impact justify planning and evidence gathering. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Planning effort should be proportional to uncertainty and impact.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: A small unambiguous low-impact change can be executed and checked directly.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Premature implementation can violate unknown dependencies and acceptance constraints.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.4&amp;variant=FV-16\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.4.html#learn-fv-16\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-17": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-17\"><summary>FV-17 · Change the hypothesis after repeated failure</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Repeating the same attempt produces the same wrong result.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-17-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-17-base-v1</small></p><p><b>An agent has twice returned a campaign report with a broken date parser. The feedback only said improve it. What feedback should be supplied next?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Accept the report because the agent says the parser is now correct.</li><li><b>B.</b> Repeat the identical vague request with stronger adjectives.</li><li><b>C.</b> Revisit the failure hypothesis and inspect the implementation or dependency evidence before another change.</li><li><b>D.</b> Provide the failing input, observed output, expected result and relevant diagnostic so refinement targets a reproducible defect.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Use the failure to test a different cause. Better feedback identifies the exact mismatch; repetition alone adds no evidence.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A date parser fails on one fixture. Supply actual input, expected result, failing assertion and relevant code; make a targeted change and re-run the fixture plus nearby cases.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The same failure survives two identical retries. Investigate a new cause, such as locale interpretation or preprocessing, instead of repeating the same prompt.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>feedback={input:03/04,locale:en_GB,expected:2026-04-03,actual:2026-03-04,check:date_locale}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Self-reported success is not verification of the failing case.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Vague repetition does not reveal which behavior must change.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Repeated failure can signal a wrong hypothesis rather than insufficient repetition. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Concrete fixtures and diagnostics define measurable refinement.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> More iterations do not imply learning. Narrow evidence and hypothesis changes matter more than request length.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.5.html#learn-fv-17\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-17-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-17-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>The same precise failing fixture still fails after two different implementations. The team is repeating its initial diagnosis without inspecting new evidence. What should change?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Revisit the failure hypothesis and inspect the implementation or dependency evidence before another change.</li><li><b>B.</b> Repeat the identical vague request with stronger adjectives.</li><li><b>C.</b> Provide the failing input, observed output, expected result and relevant diagnostic so refinement targets a reproducible defect.</li><li><b>D.</b> Accept the report because the agent says the parser is now correct.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Repeated failure can signal a wrong hypothesis rather than insufficient repetition.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Repeated failure can signal a wrong hypothesis rather than insufficient repetition.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Vague repetition does not reveal which behavior must change.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Concrete fixtures and diagnostics define measurable refinement. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Self-reported success is not verification of the failing case.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.5&amp;variant=FV-17\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.5.html#learn-fv-17\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-18": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-18\"><summary>FV-18 · Run CI with observable failure</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> An unattended review produces no useful findings because the run failed.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-18-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-18-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A nightly repository audit must inspect local files, run tests and emit a machine-readable findings artifact. It must run without a user at the terminal. Which runtime fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use a configured headless Claude Code job with scoped credentials, exit handling and validated findings extraction.</li><li><b>B.</b> Send the repository audit to Messages Batch and assume it runs the local Claude Code process.</li><li><b>C.</b> Consider Messages Batch for independent supported API requests and recover individual failed or expired items.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use interactive mode and assume a human will answer prompts in the unattended job.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> A failed review must look failed to the pipeline. An empty report cannot quietly stand for a clean review.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A scheduled repository-dependent Code review runs headless with available credentials, scoped permissions, project context, exit handling and retained artifacts. Parse its CLI envelope before validating the findings payload.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Missing credentials or malformed findings must produce an observable failure or bounded repair, never an empty “passed” report.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>ci_packet={commit,permissions,environment,prompt,exit_code,envelope,findings}; accept=all_required_checks_pass</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: The audit depends on the Code runtime and repository environment.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: API batch requests do not substitute automatically for a local Code runtime.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Independent eligible requests can be processed asynchronously; this is not a completion-time guarantee. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Unattended execution must not depend on an unavailable interactive user.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Nonblocking alone does not justify Messages Batch. JSON CLI output alone does not prove the findings schema or source accuracy.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.6.html#learn-fv-18\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-18-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-18-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A separate job summarizes 2,000 independent supplied texts, needs no local filesystem or dependent tool loop and tolerates asynchronous completion. Which runtime is eligible for consideration?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use interactive mode and assume a human will answer prompts in the unattended job.</li><li><b>B.</b> Consider Messages Batch for independent supported API requests and recover individual failed or expired items.</li><li><b>C.</b> Send the repository audit to Messages Batch and assume it runs the local Claude Code process.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use a configured headless Claude Code job with scoped credentials, exit handling and validated findings extraction.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Independent eligible requests can be processed asynchronously; this is not a completion-time guarantee.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Unattended execution must not depend on an unavailable interactive user.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Independent eligible requests can be processed asynchronously; this is not a completion-time guarantee.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: API batch requests do not substitute automatically for a local Code runtime.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: The audit depends on the Code runtime and repository environment. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=3.6&amp;variant=FV-18\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-3.6.html#learn-fv-18\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-19": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-19\"><summary>FV-19 · Define a finding with evidence criteria</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A reviewer reports too many harmless issues.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-19-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-19-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A contract scanner flags any occurrence of fee as an unexpected charge, causing false positives on clauses that expressly prohibit fees. What prompt change best addresses the defect?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Raise confidence wording without changing the decision criterion.</li><li><b>B.</b> Treat every ambiguous mention as a confirmed policy violation.</li><li><b>C.</b> Request the missing policy interpretation or label those cases unresolved before classifying them.</li><li><b>D.</b> Define the prohibited-charge criterion, exclusions, evidence citation and examples of negated versus operative clauses.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Define what counts as a finding and what must be excluded. Test those rules on borderline examples.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A legal clause review flags only an explicit unilateral termination right without the fictional required notice. Provide threshold, exclusions and source locator expectations.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> False positives cluster around historical examples. Add the exclusion and test borderline cases against actual text.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>finding_rule={trigger:unilateral_right,required:notice,evidence:clause_locator,exclude:historical_example}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Confidence phrasing does not specify what qualifies as a violation.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Ambiguity is not evidence of a confirmed violation.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: An unresolved business definition cannot be repaired by confident model wording. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Explicit operational criteria and exclusions reduce this known classification ambiguity.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> “Be careful” does not operationalize the criterion. A longer prompt without measurable boundaries can preserve the same errors.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.1.html#learn-fv-19\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-19-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-19-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>The policy owner cannot say whether conditional pass-through costs count as prohibited fees. Which next step fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Define the prohibited-charge criterion, exclusions, evidence citation and examples of negated versus operative clauses.</li><li><b>B.</b> Request the missing policy interpretation or label those cases unresolved before classifying them.</li><li><b>C.</b> Treat every ambiguous mention as a confirmed policy violation.</li><li><b>D.</b> Raise confidence wording without changing the decision criterion.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>An unresolved business definition cannot be repaired by confident model wording.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Explicit operational criteria and exclusions reduce this known classification ambiguity. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: An unresolved business definition cannot be repaired by confident model wording.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Ambiguity is not evidence of a confirmed violation.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Confidence phrasing does not specify what qualifies as a violation.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.1&amp;variant=FV-19\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.1.html#learn-fv-19\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-20": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-20\"><summary>FV-20 · Teach the edges with representative examples</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> All examples are easy successes, so the assistant mishandles missing or unusual facts.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-20-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-20-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A campaign classifier sees regular examples but fails on disclaimers and conditional promotions. Which few-shot set best addresses the observed distribution?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Add many duplicates of the already easy regular examples.</li><li><b>B.</b> Choose examples using the held-out labels, then report that same set as independent evaluation.</li><li><b>C.</b> Add examples for new product categories but leave disclaimers and conditional promotions out of the example set.</li><li><b>D.</b> Add representative contrastive examples for the failing edge cases with the intended labels and evidence.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Show representative edge cases and the expected response to missing evidence, not only more clean examples.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A finance extraction prompt includes ordinary invoices, absent tax IDs and source conflicts, with explanations of allowed nulls and unresolved values.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> All examples are clean positive invoices; the model invents rare fields. Add realistic counterexamples rather than simply adding more clean examples.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>examples=[ordinary,missing_field,conflict,credit_note]; holdout=unseen_edge_cases</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Duplicating easy cases does not address the missing edge pattern.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Using evaluation labels to tune examples contaminates the holdout.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: The observed failures are disclaimers and conditional promotions; examples that omit those cases do not teach the missing distinction.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Representative edge cases teach the distinction the current set omits.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Few-shot consistency is not a factual guarantee. Examples should represent the relevant error modes, not leak held-out answers.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.2.html#learn-fv-20\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-20-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-20-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A new market introduces a promotion structure absent from the original examples; held-out results show systematic mistakes. What should the team do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Choose examples using the held-out labels, then report that same set as independent evaluation.</li><li><b>B.</b> Add representative contrastive examples for the failing edge cases with the intended labels and evidence.</li><li><b>C.</b> Add many duplicates of the already easy regular examples.</li><li><b>D.</b> Revise or add examples for the changed distribution and evaluate on a separate held-out set.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Distribution change needs evidence-driven example revision and independent measurement.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Using evaluation labels to tune examples contaminates the holdout.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Representative edge cases teach the distinction the current set omits. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Duplicating easy cases does not address the missing edge pattern.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Distribution change needs evidence-driven example revision and independent measurement.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.2&amp;variant=FV-20\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.2.html#learn-fv-20\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-21": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-21\"><summary>FV-21 · Validate shape and truth separately</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A returned record has the right fields and types but may contain the wrong fact.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-21-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-21-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A source invoice omits a purchase-order number; the approved schema permits null for that field. What should the extractor return?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Return null for the unsupported field and preserve the source locator.</li><li><b>B.</b> Accept any non-null string because the JSON schema passes.</li><li><b>C.</b> Force every missing field to an invented realistic value.</li><li><b>D.</b> Compare the field against source evidence and correct the non-null value through semantic validation.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Check both the record’s shape and its agreement with the source. A well-formed number can still be the wrong number.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Use supported strict schema enforcement for an extraction tool, then independently validate source evidence and arithmetic. Tool selection, structural conformance and semantic validity are separate checks.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The JSON is well typed but says 900 where the source says 90. Reject the semantic mismatch and return the exact failed check.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>candidate={amount:900,currency:USD}; source={amount:90}; shape=pass; source_match=fail</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: The approved nullable contract supports absence without invention.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Schema validation cannot establish that a string is factually correct.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Filling absence with invented values creates false data.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: The wrong value has valid shape but conflicts with the source. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Forcing a tool call does not prove schema adherence unless the applicable mechanism enforces it; even conforming output can be wrong.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.3.html#learn-fv-21\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-21-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-21-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>Another invoice explicitly states PO-782, but the schema-valid output says PO-728. What validation fixes this defect?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Accept any non-null string because the JSON schema passes.</li><li><b>B.</b> Force every missing field to an invented realistic value.</li><li><b>C.</b> Compare the field against source evidence and correct the non-null value through semantic validation.</li><li><b>D.</b> Return null for the unsupported field and preserve the source locator.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>The wrong value has valid shape but conflicts with the source.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Schema validation cannot establish that a string is factually correct.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Filling absence with invented values creates false data.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: The wrong value has valid shape but conflicts with the source.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: The approved nullable contract supports absence without invention. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.3&amp;variant=FV-21\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.3.html#learn-fv-21\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-22": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-22\"><summary>FV-22 · Repair the failed check within a budget</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A correction attempt keeps failing.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-22-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-22-base-v1</small></p><p><b>An extracted invoice has line totals summing to 980 but reports 890; the source clearly shows 980. What recovery fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Choose whichever total appeared most often in prior model answers.</li><li><b>B.</b> Retry forever until any output passes a shape check.</li><li><b>C.</b> Send the exact failed arithmetic and source checks with the previous output for a bounded targeted correction.</li><li><b>D.</b> Keep the discrepancy unresolved and escalate with source evidence and attempt history.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Return the exact failed check and relevant evidence. Limit repair attempts, then hand unresolved work to the right reviewer.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> An invoice candidate’s subtotal plus tax differs from total. Return the precise values, source locators and failed arithmetic check for a bounded correction.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The retry budget is exhausted. Escalate with source, previous outputs, failed checks and unresolved discrepancy.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>failed_check={subtotal:80,tax:8,total:100,expected:88}; retry_budget=2; status=unresolved</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Model repetition is not authority for conflicting source totals.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Unbounded retries and shape-only checks do not resolve semantic failure.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: A clear check payload targets the evidenced error and bounds recovery.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: Persistent source conflict cannot be resolved by inventing agreement. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Retrying the whole document without diagnostic feedback can repeat the error or mutate correct fields.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.4.html#learn-fv-22\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-22-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-22-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>After the allowed correction attempts, two source pages still disagree about the invoice total. What should happen?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Retry forever until any output passes a shape check.</li><li><b>B.</b> Choose whichever total appeared most often in prior model answers.</li><li><b>C.</b> Keep the discrepancy unresolved and escalate with source evidence and attempt history.</li><li><b>D.</b> Send the exact failed arithmetic and source checks with the previous output for a bounded targeted correction.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Persistent source conflict cannot be resolved by inventing agreement.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Unbounded retries and shape-only checks do not resolve semantic failure.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Model repetition is not authority for conflicting source totals.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Persistent source conflict cannot be resolved by inventing agreement.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: A clear check payload targets the evidenced error and bounds recovery. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.4&amp;variant=FV-22\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.4.html#learn-fv-22\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-23": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-23\"><summary>FV-23 · Choose batch by dependencies and deadline</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Many independent jobs can wait, but some do not finish successfully.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-23-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-23-base-v1</small></p><p><b>An overnight job classifies independent supplied campaign snippets with no local runtime requirement; each can finish asynchronously. Which design fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use eligible independent batch requests with stable item IDs and per-item result reconciliation.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use an appropriate interactive or synchronous dependent workflow with deadline and escalation handling.</li><li><b>C.</b> Guarantee that every batch item will complete within 24 hours.</li><li><b>D.</b> Retry an entire successful batch whenever one item fails.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Track each job separately. Match results to IDs, preserve successes and recover only the items whose outcome requires it.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Independent eligible document requests tolerate delayed completion and have per-item recovery. Messages Batch is a candidate; retain custom IDs and item states.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Some items expire or fail. Recover those items with their recorded inputs; do not treat a submission as completed work.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>items={i1:succeeded,i2:expired,i3:errored}; recovery=[i2,i3]; deadline_policy=explicit</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Independence and asynchronous tolerance make batch a candidate.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Dependent, time-sensitive human interaction changes the runtime requirements. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: An expiry window is not a promise that all requests finish successfully.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Per-item recovery preserves successes and avoids unnecessary repeated work.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A 24-hour processing window is not a guaranteed finish time. Batch does not substitute for a local Code agent loop with filesystem state.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.5.html#learn-fv-23\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-23-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-23-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A launch approval now requires one result before constructing the next prompt, with a five-minute deadline and human clarification. What design fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Retry an entire successful batch whenever one item fails.</li><li><b>B.</b> Guarantee that every batch item will complete within 24 hours.</li><li><b>C.</b> Use an appropriate interactive or synchronous dependent workflow with deadline and escalation handling.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use eligible independent batch requests with stable item IDs and per-item result reconciliation.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Dependent, time-sensitive human interaction changes the runtime requirements.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Per-item recovery preserves successes and avoids unnecessary repeated work.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: An expiry window is not a promise that all requests finish successfully.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Dependent, time-sensitive human interaction changes the runtime requirements.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: Independence and asynchronous tolerance make batch a candidate. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.5&amp;variant=FV-23\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.5.html#learn-fv-23\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-24": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-24\"><summary>FV-24 · Give the critic independent evidence</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Two reviewers agree because both received the same misleading summary.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-24-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-24-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A consequential contract finding needs review against the source. The producer and reviewer currently share only the producer’s conclusion. What architecture best improves review?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use majority agreement as proof even when reviewers share the same missing source.</li><li><b>B.</b> Ask the producer to agree with its own conclusion twice.</li><li><b>C.</b> Use the deterministic validator and bounded human or automated spot checks appropriate to the low risk.</li><li><b>D.</b> Give an independent reviewer the source, producer artifact and explicit criteria; preserve and resolve disagreements.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Agreement is weak proof if both reviewers share the same missing evidence. Give an independent check the original source or a different test.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A producer proposes extracted claims; a separate critic checks source locators, omissions and contradictions under explicit criteria. Resolve disagreement by evidence.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Both instances share a misleading summary and agree. Give the critic original sources or a different check; agreement alone is not validation.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>producer={claims,sources}; critic={criteria,original_sources,failed_checks}; dispute={claim,source,verdict}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Shared blind spots can survive agreement without supporting evidence.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Repeated self-agreement is not independent review.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Additional model passes need to justify their cost and failure exposure for this bounded task. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Independent source-based critique can identify producer omissions.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Repeated identical prompts can amplify the same blind spot. Independence needs evidence and criteria, not just another instance.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.6.html#learn-fv-24\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-24-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-24-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A low-risk internal formatting task has an exact deterministic validator; no substantive conclusion is produced. What review approach is proportionate?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use majority agreement as proof even when reviewers share the same missing source.</li><li><b>B.</b> Give an independent reviewer the source, producer artifact and explicit criteria; preserve and resolve disagreements.</li><li><b>C.</b> Ask the producer to agree with its own conclusion twice.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use the deterministic validator and bounded human or automated spot checks appropriate to the low risk.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Additional model passes need to justify their cost and failure exposure for this bounded task.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Shared blind spots can survive agreement without supporting evidence.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Independent source-based critique can identify producer omissions. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Repeated self-agreement is not independent review.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Additional model passes need to justify their cost and failure exposure for this bounded task.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=4.6&amp;variant=FV-24\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-4.6.html#learn-fv-24\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-25": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-25\"><summary>FV-25 · Compact the decision state, not just prose</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A shorter conversation summary drops an important exception.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-25-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-25-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A long investigation is approaching its context limit. A rare jurisdiction exception determines the final action. What should the compact state preserve?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Keep only the overall conclusion and discard exceptions to save space.</li><li><b>B.</b> Reacquire the revised source and revalidate the affected decision instead of reconstructing missing details.</li><li><b>C.</b> Treat the compact summary as permanently authoritative after external changes.</li><li><b>D.</b> Preserve the exception, decisions, unresolved issues and precise source pointers in the compact state.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Preserve facts that change the decision, including restrictions and unresolved questions. A shorter story must not change the rules.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A long marketing investigation stores the current question, accepted constraints, unresolved issues, source pointers and next bounded action before compaction.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A summary drops the “do not contact” exception. Restore it from the source before proceeding; never infer that omitted means revoked.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>state={question,constraints:[no_contact],sources:[CRM42],unresolved:[consent_age],next:verify}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Discarding a decisive exception can change the correct action.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: A changed source requires fresh evidence even when conversation state persists. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Summaries do not freeze external policy state.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Critical constraints and pointers must survive compaction to support later decisions.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Keeping every transcript token can overwhelm context. A compact record must retain decisive exceptions and reacquisition paths.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.1.html#learn-fv-25\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-25-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-25-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>After compaction, a referenced policy document is revised and the summary no longer establishes the applicable exception. What should happen?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Keep only the overall conclusion and discard exceptions to save space.</li><li><b>B.</b> Reacquire the revised source and revalidate the affected decision instead of reconstructing missing details.</li><li><b>C.</b> Preserve the exception, decisions, unresolved issues and precise source pointers in the compact state.</li><li><b>D.</b> Treat the compact summary as permanently authoritative after external changes.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A changed source requires fresh evidence even when conversation state persists.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Discarding a decisive exception can change the correct action.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: A changed source requires fresh evidence even when conversation state persists.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: Critical constraints and pointers must survive compaction to support later decisions. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Summaries do not freeze external policy state.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.1&amp;variant=FV-25\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.1.html#learn-fv-25\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-26": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-26\"><summary>FV-26 · Honor explicit escalation with context</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A case transfers to a person with a misleading action status.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-26-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-26-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A customer explicitly asks to speak to a human about disputed campaign billing. Policy permits that escalation. What should the assistant do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Escalate promptly with the issue, verified identifiers, evidence and unresolved status.</li><li><b>B.</b> Ask a targeted clarification under the support policy while keeping escalation available.</li><li><b>C.</b> Escalate every ambiguous comment without first applying the stated clarification policy.</li><li><b>D.</b> Continue forcing self-service after the explicit human request.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Pass what is known, what was attempted, what really happened and what remains blocked. Proposed and completed are different states.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A customer explicitly asks for a human. Route promptly with verified identity status, issues, evidence, attempted and blocked actions, and next owner.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> The handoff says credit issued, but only a proposal exists. Correct status and include the operation ledger before transferring.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>handoff={identity_status,issues,confirmed,proposed,blocked,reason,next_owner}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: The explicit request is a defined escalation trigger.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Ambiguity without the trigger should follow the applicable clarification route. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Automatic escalation of every ambiguity bypasses the specified support workflow.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Ignoring an explicit supported request defeats the stated escalation policy.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A transcript dump does not ensure an actionable handoff; a vague complaint is not identical to an explicit human request.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.2.html#learn-fv-26\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-26-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-26-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A customer says the report is confusing but does not request a human; policy calls for clarification of the disputed metric first. What should happen?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Ask a targeted clarification under the support policy while keeping escalation available.</li><li><b>B.</b> Escalate every ambiguous comment without first applying the stated clarification policy.</li><li><b>C.</b> Escalate promptly with the issue, verified identifiers, evidence and unresolved status.</li><li><b>D.</b> Continue forcing self-service after the explicit human request.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Ambiguity without the trigger should follow the applicable clarification route.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Ambiguity without the trigger should follow the applicable clarification route.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Automatic escalation of every ambiguity bypasses the specified support workflow.</li><li><b>C:</b> Nearest alternative: The explicit request is a defined escalation trigger. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Ignoring an explicit supported request defeats the stated escalation policy.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.2&amp;variant=FV-26\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.2.html#learn-fv-26\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-27": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-27\"><summary>FV-27 · Propagate partial success without hiding failure</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> One helper succeeds and another required helper fails.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-27-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-27-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A coordinator receives valid findings from two specialists and a retryable source timeout from a third. The missing source is mandatory for the final conclusion. What should it do?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Invent the missing specialist’s findings so the final answer looks complete.</li><li><b>B.</b> Return the permitted partial overview with attributed findings and an explicit unavailable-source limitation.</li><li><b>C.</b> Discard every successful result and restart all specialists.</li><li><b>D.</b> Preserve successful findings, report the missing mandatory evidence and dispatch targeted recovery before a final supported conclusion.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Keep the successful evidence but show the missing part. Recover the required evidence or limit the conclusion; do not fill the gap with a guess.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Specialists return findings and structured failures to the coordinator. Preserve successful evidence and decide which missing dimensions block the final claim.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> A mandatory permissions source fails. Target that recovery or stop the affected conclusion; do not replace its facts with another specialist’s guess.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>result={dimension:permissions,status:failed,reason:access_denied,retryable:false}; aggregate=partial</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Missing evidence must not be replaced with fabricated findings.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Explicit permission for partial coverage changes whether a bounded output is acceptable. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Unrelated successful work need not be discarded for one isolated failure.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Targeted recovery retains valid work while respecting the mandatory evidence requirement.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> Discarding all successes wastes evidence; silently dropping a failed mandatory dimension fabricates completeness.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.3.html#learn-fv-27\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-27-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-27-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A different brief explicitly permits a partial overview; an optional source is unavailable and cannot be recovered now. Which output fits?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Return the permitted partial overview with attributed findings and an explicit unavailable-source limitation.</li><li><b>B.</b> Preserve successful findings, report the missing mandatory evidence and dispatch targeted recovery before a final supported conclusion.</li><li><b>C.</b> Discard every successful result and restart all specialists.</li><li><b>D.</b> Invent the missing specialist’s findings so the final answer looks complete.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Explicit permission for partial coverage changes whether a bounded output is acceptable.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Correct: Explicit permission for partial coverage changes whether a bounded output is acceptable.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Targeted recovery retains valid work while respecting the mandatory evidence requirement. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Unrelated successful work need not be discarded for one isolated failure.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Missing evidence must not be replaced with fabricated findings.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.3&amp;variant=FV-27\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.3.html#learn-fv-27\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-28": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-28\"><summary>FV-28 · Build a minimal code evidence ledger</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> The code investigation grows until the important exception is lost.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-28-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-28-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A defect appears in one generated campaign URL among a large codebase. What exploration strategy fits before editing?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Expand into the demonstrated dependency chain and inspect affected callers before choosing the repair.</li><li><b>B.</b> Search narrowly, read relevant files and keep a compact evidence ledger of paths, dependencies and findings.</li><li><b>C.</b> Load the entire repository into context before forming any hypothesis.</li><li><b>D.</b> Use a stale summary to guess the helper’s current implementation.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Keep a small map of the files and facts needed for the change. Re-read current code when a lost detail could affect the decision.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A large codebase bug starts with targeted symbol search, relevant caller reads and a file/dependency ledger. Expand only the evidence path needed for the defect.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Compaction loses a function’s exception condition. Re-read the actual file at the current commit before editing.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>ledger={commit,files:[handler,caller],symbols:[retry],unresolved:[ownership],next:test_contract}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Newly evidenced cross-cutting dependencies require appropriately expanded investigation. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Targeted evidence reduces context noise and supports a justified local diagnosis.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Unbounded loading can dilute attention without establishing relevant evidence.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: A stale summary cannot replace reading the current implementation.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A guessed summary cannot replace current code evidence. Reading the whole repository also makes the decisive detail harder to retain.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.4.html#learn-fv-28\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-28-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-28-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A targeted read shows that the URL builder depends on a shared encoding helper used by three services. What should exploration do next?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use a stale summary to guess the helper’s current implementation.</li><li><b>B.</b> Load the entire repository into context before forming any hypothesis.</li><li><b>C.</b> Expand into the demonstrated dependency chain and inspect affected callers before choosing the repair.</li><li><b>D.</b> Search narrowly, read relevant files and keep a compact evidence ledger of paths, dependencies and findings.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>Newly evidenced cross-cutting dependencies require appropriately expanded investigation.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: A stale summary cannot replace reading the current implementation.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Unbounded loading can dilute attention without establishing relevant evidence.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Newly evidenced cross-cutting dependencies require appropriately expanded investigation.</li><li><b>D:</b> Nearest alternative: Targeted evidence reduces context noise and supports a justified local diagnosis. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.4&amp;variant=FV-28\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.4.html#learn-fv-28\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-29": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-29\"><summary>FV-29 · Test risk slices and review capacity</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> A quality score looks good overall but hides risky mistakes or an overloaded review queue.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-29-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-29-base-v1</small></p><p><b>A routing model is 98% accurate overall but only 65% accurate on a rare high-loss claim category. What should review routing use?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Define and test a capacity and overflow policy with observable escalation for unresolved review demand.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use stratified error and loss evidence to route the high-risk category rather than rely on the overall average.</li><li><b>C.</b> Accept model confidence as calibrated without checking it against outcomes.</li><li><b>D.</b> Treat the overall 98% as sufficient evidence that every category is ready.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Test important groups separately and check what reviewers can actually handle. A changed input population can make yesterday’s threshold unreliable.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> A finance extractor has high overall accuracy but weaker rare-currency performance. Evaluate severity-weighted slices on independent holdout and calibrate review thresholds with actual human capacity.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> Distribution drift breaks calibration. Recalibrate on suitable data and evaluate on a separate holdout; do not tune against the final test set.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>metrics={overall:0.98,rare_currency:0.70}; capacity=20; queued=35; overflow=hold</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: A review design must specify what happens when its required human queue exceeds capacity. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Correct: Aggregate accuracy can hide consequential subgroup failure.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: Expressed confidence requires empirical calibration and monitoring.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: An average does not establish subgroup performance or loss tolerance.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> The average can hide dangerous categories; confidence scores alone are not calibrated correctness probabilities.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.5.html#learn-fv-29\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-29-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-29-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>The routing team has calibrated scores but reviewer demand exceeds daily capacity; a published policy forbids silently dropping flagged claims. What is required?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Treat the overall 98% as sufficient evidence that every category is ready.</li><li><b>B.</b> Use stratified error and loss evidence to route the high-risk category rather than rely on the overall average.</li><li><b>C.</b> Define and test a capacity and overflow policy with observable escalation for unresolved review demand.</li><li><b>D.</b> Accept model confidence as calibrated without checking it against outcomes.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A review design must specify what happens when its required human queue exceeds capacity.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: An average does not establish subgroup performance or loss tolerance.</li><li><b>B:</b> Nearest alternative: Aggregate accuracy can hide consequential subgroup failure. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: A review design must specify what happens when its required human queue exceeds capacity.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: Expressed confidence requires empirical calibration and monitoring.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.5&amp;variant=FV-29\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.5.html#learn-fv-29\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>", "FV-30": "<details class=\"layer-unit\" id=\"layer-fv-30\"><summary>FV-30 · Keep sources through a join</summary><p><b>Picture the problem:</b> Two systems report different facts about the same subject.</p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-30-base-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-30-base-v1</small></p><p><b>Two systems disagree about a contract’s termination date and have different update timestamps. What should the synthesis preserve?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Use federation if appropriate for access while retaining provenance and unresolved consistency checks.</li><li><b>B.</b> Select the newer date automatically without knowing which source is authoritative for that field.</li><li><b>C.</b> Keep field-level source locators, timestamps and the explicit discrepancy without inventing a merged date.</li><li><b>D.</b> Assume one query interface guarantees consistent underlying records.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-hint\"><summary>What is this asking? — plain-language hint</summary><p>Which option meets the exact requirement in the question? Ask what must be known, what may run, and what the result would prove. Choose before opening the explanation.</p></details><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen — explain the decision and show the next layer</summary><p><b>In everyday language:</b> Keep each fact attached to its source, time and scope. Do not erase a disagreement by joining records under one name.</p><p><b>A separate worked application of the same mechanism:</b> Separate finance and CRM interfaces return revenue and customer records with timestamps and field-level provenance. Join only under a declared identifier rule.</p><p><b>What breaks in that companion example:</b> CRM says customer active while finance reports suspended at a different date. Preserve both assertions and their scope; ask for policy or current authoritative evidence.</p><p><b>Read the artifact:</b> This illustrative decision-state record belongs to the companion example above, not the exact practice question. It is not a complete runnable SDK request. Read the field names as the facts that application must keep separate.</p><pre><code>row={customer_id:C7,revenue:{value:120,source:FIN9,as_of:d1},status:{value:active,source:CRM4,as_of:d2}}</code></pre><p><b>Why these choices:</b></p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Nearest alternative: Federation can simplify access but does not establish truth or agreement. However, that option addresses the paired case; it does not satisfy this stem’s decisive constraint.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: Recency alone may not determine field authority or effective contractual terms.</li><li><b>C:</b> Correct: Attribution and explicit uncertainty preserve the actual evidence state.</li><li><b>D:</b> Incorrect: A unified interface does not erase source conflicts.</li></ul><p><b>Reject the close alternative:</b> A joined table can erase provenance and make mismatched-age sources look consistent. Federation does not establish truth by itself.</p><p><b>Replay and prove:</b> Use the concrete failure above as the test input. Explain the recorded state before the failure, the check that changes the next action, and the receipt, source or error that must remain visible. Then trace the relevant diagram and proof in the <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.6.html#learn-fv-30\">full objective episode</a>; a proposed repair is not execution evidence.</p></details><p><b>Change the situation — choose again before opening the explanation.</b></p><div class=\"layer-question\" data-assessment-exposure=\"cct-fv-30-retry-v1\"><p><small>Original exam-style practice · Choose 1 · cct-fv-30-retry-v1</small></p><p><b>A supported federation provides one query interface. The coordinator receives conflicting termination dates with source locators removed. What return contract must the research subagent provide before synthesis?</b></p><ul class=\"layer-options\"><li><b>A.</b> Select the newer date automatically without knowing which source is authoritative for that field.</li><li><b>B.</b> Assume one query interface guarantees consistent underlying records.</li><li><b>C.</b> Report both possible dates in prose, leaving the coordinator to guess which source supports each date.</li><li><b>D.</b> Return a structured claim-to-source mapping with document locators, relevant evidence and timestamps, retaining the conflict for the coordinator.</li></ul></div><details class=\"layer-answer\"><summary>I have chosen again — compare the changed decision</summary><p>A unified query interface does not replace structured claim-source mappings. Downstream synthesis needs attributable claims and explicit conflict, not inferred source identity.</p><ul><li><b>A:</b> Incorrect: Recency alone may not determine field authority or effective contractual terms.</li><li><b>B:</b> Incorrect: A unified interface does not erase source conflicts.</li><li><b>C:</b> Incorrect: A prose discrepancy without claim-source identity cannot preserve provenance through downstream synthesis.</li><li><b>D:</b> Correct: Explicit claim-source mappings preserve provenance through a coordinator and avoid invented reconciliation.</li></ul></details><p><a href=\"practice/index.html?exam=CCAR-F&amp;objective=5.6&amp;variant=FV-30\">Practise the base decision</a> · <a href=\"lessons/ccar-f/objectives/objective-5.6.html#learn-fv-30\">Open the complete objective and drawing</a></p></details>"};
    document.querySelectorAll('.layer-coaching[data-layer-root]').forEach(box => {
        const picker = box.querySelector('.layer-picker');
        const stage = box.querySelector('.layer-stage');
        const saved = new Map();
        let current = null;
        function show() {
            if (current) saved.set(current, stage.firstElementChild);
            const next = picker.value;
            const existing = saved.get(next);
            stage.replaceChildren();
            if (existing) stage.append(existing);
            else {
                const template = document.createElement('template');
                template.innerHTML = panels[next] || '';
                const panel = template.content.firstElementChild;
                if (!panel) return;
                panel.open = true;
                panel.querySelectorAll('a[href]').forEach(a => {
                    const href = a.getAttribute('href');
                    if (!/^(https?:|#)/.test(href)) a.setAttribute('href', box.dataset.layerRoot + href);
                });
                stage.append(panel);
            }
            current = next;
            // Mark only the selected scene as exposed. Keep all attempts, scores and active sessions.
            try {
                const key = 'claude-cert-practice.v1';
                const state = JSON.parse(localStorage.getItem(key) || '{}');
                state.q = state.q || {};
                stage.querySelectorAll('[data-assessment-exposure]').forEach(el => {
                    const id = el.dataset.assessmentExposure;
                    const record = state.q[id] || (state.q[id] = {seen:0,right:0});
                    record.exposed = true;
                    record.exposure_reason = 'Opened this original practice scene in local teaching';
                });
                localStorage.setItem(key, JSON.stringify(state));
            } catch (e) { /* Reading remains available when browser storage is unavailable. */ }

        }
        box.addEventListener('toggle', () => { if (box.open && !current) show(); });
        picker.addEventListener('change', show);
    });
})();
